this is one of the most important screens because it demonstrates the execution of the Sextant Protocol. It is no longer just navigation—it shows the system processing a scenario and explaining the result.
You can document it in the Operational Manual as follows.
Self-Test & Cascade Engine Screen
Operational Manual Section
1. Purpose
The Self-Test & Cascade Engine verifies that the Sextant system is operational before executing a resilience scenario. It confirms system readiness, runs diagnostic checks, executes the selected scenario, and provides an explainable assessment of cascading effects.
This screen supports transparency by showing not only the calculated risk, but also the reasoning behind the result.
2. Screen Components
RUN SCENARIO
Starts the selected resilience simulation.
Functions:
Loads the chosen scenario
Initiates the simulation workflow
Sends data to the Cascade Engine for processing
RUN SELF TEST
Performs a system readiness check before simulation.
Checks may include:
Engine availability
Module readiness
Scenario loading
Runtime integrity
System status verification
CASCADE ENGINE
Displays the execution status of the simulation engine.
Example:
SELF TEST STARTING...
→ CONFIDENCE_LOSS
🟢 SELF TEST PASSED
This indicates that:
The self-test completed successfully.
The selected scenario is CONFIDENCE_LOSS.
The system is ready to continue with the simulation.
RISK ENGINE
Example:
79 (HIGH)
The Risk Engine calculates the overall simulated risk level based on interactions between the monitored system nodes.
Illustrative interpretation:
0–20: Stable
21–40: Watch
41–60: Stress
61–80: High Risk
81–100: Critical
TIMELINE
Displays the sequence of operational events.
Example:
BOOT TRACE...
This provides a chronological record of system activities during the simulation.
SYSTEM STATE
Example:
SYSTEM ONLINE
Confirms that the platform remains operational throughout the simulation.
EXPLANATION LAYER
Example:
CASCADE ANALYSIS

Drivers:
currency instability
→ liquidity contraction
→ banking stress
→ confidence erosion

Severity:
critical
This is the Explainability Layer of Sextant.
Rather than presenting only a numerical risk score, the system explains how the simulated cascade developed, allowing operators to understand the chain of events that contributed to the outcome.
3. Operational Workflow
Run Self Test
        ↓
Verify System Readiness
        ↓
Run Scenario
        ↓
Cascade Engine Processing
        ↓
Risk Engine Assessment
        ↓
Timeline Recording
        ↓
System State Verification
        ↓
Explanation Layer Output
4. Operational Role
This screen demonstrates the transition from system validation to scenario execution and finally to explainable resilience analysis.
It combines:
System readiness verification
Scenario execution
Risk assessment
Event logging
Explainable cascade analysThishis section is particularly valuable for institutional users because it shows not only the simulation result, but also the reasoning process behind the assessment, supporting transparency and review.
